title: What Happens If You Hold Your Breath?
voice: am_michael
speed: 1.0
lead: 0.35
outro: 1.0
gap: 0.26
music: tension
sources[4]: Healthline, 'What happens when you hold your breath' (CO2 build-up drives the urge to breathe; involuntary breathing movements; fainting, after which breathing resumes), "UBC Science Creative Quarterly, 'Waiting to inhale: why it hurts to hold your breath' (CO2 sensing, diaphragm contractions)", Redwoods Group, 'Breath holding and shallow water blackout' (passing out underwater can be fatal), "Mammalian diving reflex: breath-holding slows the heart rate (bradycardia), most strongly with the face in cold water"
lines[10]:
  - text, caps
  - What happens if you hold your breath for as long as you can?, What *happens | if you *hold | your *breath?
  - "For the first thirty seconds, nothing much.", The first | *30 seconds | nothing *much
  - "Your lungs are full, and your blood is still carrying plenty of oxygen.", Lungs are *full | your *blood | still has *oxygen
  - "But carbon dioxide starts building up,", Carbon *dioxide | starts *building up
  - "and that, not the lack of oxygen, is what makes you desperate to breathe.", "That, *not | lack of *oxygen | makes you *desperate | to *breathe"
  - "Your diaphragm starts twitching, trying to force a breath,", Your *diaphragm | starts *twitching | trying to *force | a *breath
  - and your heart actually slows down to save oxygen., Your *heart | slows *down | to save *oxygen
  - "Push even further, and your brain simply switches you off.", Push even *further | your *brain | switches you *off
  - "You pass out, and your body starts breathing again on its own.", You pass *out | your *body | *breathing again | on its *own
  - Which is why you should never try this underwater., Which is *why | you should *never | try this *underwater
